{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-804be3f0d30b458f", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tarXiv:2608.09088v1 [cs.AI] 10 Aug 2026\n2\t\n3\tA Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic\n4\tFusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\n5\tStefanos Gkikas\n6\t\n7\tYang Guo\n8\t\n9\tGuangliang Li\n10\t\n11\tHonda Research Institute Japan\n12\tWako City, Japan\n13\tstefanos.gkikas@jp.honda-ri.com\n14\t\n15\tFaculty of Information Science\n16\tand Engineering\n17\tOcean University of China\n18\tQingdao, China\n19\tdiw85827@gmail.com\n20\t\n21\tFaculty of Information Science\n22\tand Engineering\n23\tOcean University of China\n24\tQingdao, China\n25\tguangliangli@ouc.edu.cn\n26\t\n27\tRaul Fernandez Rojas\n28\t\n29\tGiorgos Giannakakis\n30\t\n31\tRandy Gomez\n32\t\n33\tBioSIS (Biosensing & Intelligent Systems) Lab\n34\tCentre for Intelligent Computing and Systems\n35\tUniversity of Canberra\n36\tCanberra, Australia\n37\traul.fernandezrojas@canberra.edu.au\n38\t\n39\tDepartment of Electronic Engineering\n40\tHellenic Mediterranean University\n41\tChania, Greece\n42\tggian@hmu.gr\n43\t\n44\tHonda Research Institute Japan\n45\tWako City, Japan\n46\tr.gomez@jp.honda-ri.com\n47\t\n48\tThe global burden of mental health conditions is substantial.\n49\tEstimates attribute an additional 53 million cases of major\n50\tdepressive disorder and 76 million cases of anxiety disorder to\n51\tthe COVID-19 pandemic in a single year [6]. Mixed affective\n52\tstates are clinically important because they preserve the simultaneous presence of opposing affective components, information\n53\tthat single-valence descriptors discard [7]. Evidence from psychotherapy populations further shows that concurrent positive\n54\tand negative affect can precede improvements in psychological\n55\twell-being, highlighting the value of characterizing mixed\n56\temotional experience for mental health monitoring [8].\n57\tSelf-report alone is limited in settings that require continuous\n58\tand unobtrusive monitoring. EEG frequency-band features have\n59\tbeen studied across psychiatric conditions such as depression,\n60\tbipolar disorder, anxiety, and post-traumatic stress disorder [9].\n61\tWearable systems that apply machine learning to physiological\n62\tsignals have also shown promise for the passive detection\n63\tof anxiety and depression [10]. Among physiological modalities, EEG directly measures central nervous system activity\n64\twith millisecond resolution, capturing affective dynamics that\n65\tperipheral biosignals cannot express in the same way [11].\n66\tI. I NTRODUCTION\n67\tAutomatic emotion recognition from EEG has advanced\n68\tMixed emotions refer to emotional episodes in which positive considerably with deep learning, especially for discrete afand negative affective components occur at the same time [1]. fective classification tasks [12]. Convolutional and recurrent\n69\tThey are not adequately described by a single valence axis. architectures have been applied to spectral and temporal\n70\tInstead, they form a distinct affective category that requires the EEG representations [13], while attention-based models have\n71\ttwo poles to be evaluated independently [2]. Instruments such enabled longer-range dependencies to be modeled within\n72\tas the Positive and Negative Affect Schedule [3] and valence- EEG sequences [14]. Affective responses, however, do not\n73\tarousal-dominance ratings [4] are commonly used to assess evolve at a single temporal resolution. They include both\n74\tsubjective affective experience. However, reconstructing the short, transient reactions and sustained dynamics throughout\n75\tconflicting components of a mixed affective state after the event the full stimulus duration. The analysis window therefore\n76\tis difficult, and retrospective self-report becomes unreliable remains a central design choice, as it determines the temporal\n77\twhen affective states must be monitored continuously [5].\n78\tinformation exposed to the model [11]. Mixed emotional states,\n79\tAbstract—Mixed emotions represent a clinically relevant but\n80\tstill underexplored target for automatic emotion recognition. EEG\n81\tprovides millisecond-level access to neural activity, yet most EEG\n82\tpipelines analyze the signal through a single temporal window,\n83\tthereby fixing the temporal structure available to the model. This\n84\tstudy introduces a multi-scale temporal framework for EEGbased emotion recognition. The EEG waveform is decomposed\n85\tinto windows of one or several durations, processed by a shared\n86\tattention-based encoder, and integrated through a dynamic fusion\n87\tmodule that assigns sample-specific weights across temporal\n88\tscales. The framework is evaluated under a subject-independent\n89\tprotocol in binary and three-class settings, with the three-class\n90\ttask including the mixed affective category. The best results are\n91\t65.22% for the two-class task and 45.43% for the three-class task.\n92\tBoth are obtained with three-scale dynamic-fusion configurations\n93\tand remain substantially above the full-signal baseline. The bestperforming temporal scales differ between the two tasks. Dynamic\n94\tfusion outperforms concatenation in the highest-scoring two-class\n95\tconfiguration and slightly exceeds it in the highest-scoring threeclass configuration, although these multi-scale settings require\n96\tsubstantially more computation than the full-signal baseline.\n97\tIndex Terms—Electroencephalogram, emotion assessment, affective computing, attention\n98\t\n99\t\fwhere positive and negative components co-occur, remain\n100\tcomparatively underexplored from a computational perspective\n101\tdespite their clinical relevance [1].\n102\tThis study proposes a multi-scale temporal framework for\n103\tEEG-based emotion recognition. Time-domain EEG waveforms\n104\tare decomposed into windows of multiple durations, encoded\n105\tby a shared attention-based model, and integrated through\n106\ta dynamic fusion mechanism that assigns sample-adaptive\n107\tweights across temporal scales. The framework is evaluated in\n108\tbinary and three-class classification settings covering positive,\n109\tnegative, and mixed affective categories.\n110\t\n111\tdataset and the affective dimension, with optimal windows\n112\tranging from a few seconds to more than ten seconds [28].\n113\tBeyond fixed-window analysis, multi-scale architectures have\n114\tbeen developed to represent short-term and sustained EEG\n115\tdynamics within a single model. Multi-scale convolutional and\n116\tgraph-based models encode EEG activity at multiple temporal\n117\tresolutions [29], [30], based on the premise that affective\n118\tprocesses unfold across multiple temporal scales. A related\n119\tmulti-window fusion strategy has also been explored for pain\n120\trecognition from respiratory signals, where a single crossattention transformer aggregates representations extracted at\n121\tdifferent temporal resolutions [31]. Unlike that approach, which\n122\tapplies multi-window modeling to a single respiration signal\n123\tII. R ELATED W ORK\n124\tfor pain recognition, the present framework operates directly\n125\tAutomatic emotion recognition has been studied across phys- on multichannel EEG waveforms, processes variable-length\n126\tiological and behavioral modalities, including electrodermal windows through a shared multi-layer segment-based encoder,\n127\tactivity, photoplethysmography, cardiovascular signals, and and uses sample-adaptive fusion to weight temporal scales and\n128\tfacial video [15]. EEG occupies a particular role in this setting positions for each recording. Despite these developments, most\n129\tbecause it records central nervous system activity at millisecond EEG-based emotion recognition studies still focus on standard\n130\tresolution and provides access to affective correlates that discrete or dimensional categories. The 