enation\n919\tand 45.43% under dynamic fusion, the highest test accuracy\n920\tfor this task. Unlike the two-class setting, the highest-scoring\n921\tthree-class configurations do not include the full-signal window.\n922\tUnder the present DMER evaluation, this suggests a stronger\n923\trole for temporally local information, possibly because positive\n924\tand negative affective components emerge within shorter or\n925\tpartially overlapping intervals.\n926\tD. Computational Cost\n927\tTable IV reports the computational cost for the 15 unique\n928\twindow configurations. The parameter count remains between\n929\t4.81 and 4.97 M, reflecting the shared-encoder design. The\n930\tsame encoder processes every window, so the main encoder\n931\tparameter count is independent of the number of extracted\n932\twindows. The small variation across configurations comes\n933\tfrom the fusion head, whose input dimensionality scales with\n934\tthe total number of windows N , and from the window-lengthspecific normalization layers.\n935\tGFLOPs vary more strongly with the window configuration, from 1.36 GFLOPs for the full-signal baseline to\n936\t17.88 GFLOPs for the four-scale setting. Configurations with\n937\tseveral short windows are the most expensive because the\n938\tnumber of encoded window instances increases with N , even\n939\tthough windows of the same duration are batched within the\n940\tshared encoder. The highest-scoring configurations do not use\n941\tthe maximum-cost four-scale setting, although both remain\n942\tcomputationally demanding. The {full, 2, 10} configuration\n943\trequires 13.65 GFLOPs, while the {2, 5, 10} configuration\n944\trequires 16.52 GFLOPs, the second-highest cost among the\n945\tevaluated settings. Fig. 2 shows the accuracy-cost trade-off for\n946\tall 15 configurations under dynamic fusion.\n947\tE. Overall Analysis & Discussion\n948\tThe proposed multi-scale temporal framework reaches its\n949\tbest results when multiple temporal scales ar