e:\n1474\tReorganizing Facial Spatiotemporal Representations for Improved Pain\n1475\tAssessment,” in 2026 14th International Conference on Affective Computing and Intelligent Interaction (ACII). IEEE, 2026.\n1476\t[26] H. Candra, M. Yuwono, R. Chai, A. Handojoseno, I. Elamvazuthi, H. T.\n1477\tNguyen, and S. Su, “Investigation of window size in classification of\n1478\tEEG-emotion signal with wavelet entropy and support vector machine,”\n1479\tin 2015 37th Annual International Conference of the IEEE Engineering\n1480\tin Medicine and Biology Society (EMBC), 2015, pp. 7250–7253.\n1481\t[27] P. Keelawat, N. Thammasan, M. Numao, and B. Kijsirikul, “A comparative study of window size and channel arrangement on EEG-Emotion\n1482\trecognition using deep CNN,” Sensors, vol. 21, no. 5, p. 1678, 2021.\n1483\t[28] D. Ouyang, Y. Yuan, G. Li, and Z. Guo, “The effect of time window\n1484\tlength on EEG-Based emotion recognition,” Sensors, vol. 22, no. 13, p.\n1485\t4939, 2022.\n1486\t[29] X. Zhou, D. Huang, X. Peng, and L. Yin, “miMamba: EEG-Based\n1487\temotion recognition with multi-scale inverted mamba models,” IEEE\n1488\tTransactions on Affective Computing, vol. 16, no. 4, pp. 3266–3278,\n1489\t2025.\n1490\t[30] H. Liu, Y. Gong, Z. Yan, Z. Zhuang, and J. Lu, “MSGM: A multi-scale\n1491\tspatiotemporal graph mamba for EEG emotion recognition,” Frontiers\n1492\tin Neuroscience, vol. 20, p. 1665145, 2026.\n1493\t[31] S. Gkikas, I. Kyprakis, and M. Tsiknakis, “Efficient pain recognition via\n1494\trespiration signals: A single cross-attention transformer multi-window\n1495\tfusion pipeline,” in Companion Proceedings of the 27th International\n1496\tConference on Multimodal Interaction, ser. ICMI Companion ’25. New\n1497\tYork, NY, USA: Association for Computing Machinery, 2025, pp. 70–79.\n1498\t[32] P. Yang, N. Liu, X. Liu, Y. Shu, W. Ji, Z. Ren, J. Sheng, M. Yu, R. Yi,\n1499\tD. Zhang, and Y.-J. Liu, “A multimodal dataset for mixed emotion\n1500\trecognition,” Scientific Data, vol. 11, no. 1, p. 847, 2024.\n1501\t\n1502\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "cb873794-e027-4571-97d5-338443188b58", "uuid": "0035561f-7bb0-4e85-9e5e-aa8d1232b72b", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T17:48:43.969Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.09088v1.txt", "content": "arXiv:2608.09088v1 [cs.AI] 10 Aug 2026\n\nA Multi-Scale Temporal Framework with Dynamic\nFusion for EEG-Based Emotion Recognition\nStefanos Gkikas\n\nYang Guo\n\nGuangliang Li\n\nHonda Research Institute Japan\nWako City, Japan\nstefanos.gkikas@jp.honda-ri.com\n\nFaculty of Information Science\nand Engineering\nOcean University of China\nQingdao, China\ndiw85827@gmail.com\n\nFaculty of Information Science\nand Engineering\nOcean University of China\nQingdao, China\nguangliangli@ouc.edu.cn\n\nRaul Fernandez Rojas\n\nGiorgos Giannakakis\n\nRandy Gomez\n\nBioSIS (Biosensing & Intelligent Systems) Lab\nCentre for Intelligent Computing and Systems\nUniversity of Canberra\nCanberra, Australia\nraul.fernandezrojas@canberra.edu.au\n\nDepartment of Electronic Engineering\nHellenic Mediterranean University\nChania, Greece\nggian@hmu.gr\n\nHonda Research Institute Japan\nWako City, Japan\nr.gomez@jp.honda-ri.com\n\nThe global burden of mental health conditions is substantial.\nEstimates attribute an additional 53 million cases of major\ndepressive disorder and 76 million cases of anxiety disorder to\nthe COVID-19 pandemic in a single year [6]. Mixed affective\nstates are clinically important because they preserve the simultaneous presence of opposing affective components, information\nthat single-valence descriptors discard [7]. Evidence from psychotherapy populations further shows that concurrent positive\nand negative affect can precede improvements in psychological\nwell-being, highlighting the value of characterizing mixed\nemotional experience for mental health monitoring [8].\nSelf-report alone is limited in settings that require continuous\nand unobtrusive monitoring. EEG frequency-band features have\nbeen studied across psychiatric conditions such as depression,\nbipolar disorder, anxiety, and post-traumatic stress disorder [9].\nWearable systems that apply machine learning to physiological\nsignals have also shown promise for the passive detection\nof anxiety and depression [10]. Among physiological modalities, EEG directly measures central nervous system activity\nwith millisecond resolution, capturing affective dynamics that\nperipheral biosignals cannot express in the same way [11].\nI. I NTRODUCTION\nAutomatic emotion recognition from EEG has advanced\nMixed emotions refer to emotional episodes in which positive considerably with deep learning, especially for discrete afand negative affective components occur at the same time [1]. fective classification tasks [12]. Convolutional and recurrent\nThey are not adequately described by a single valence axis. architectures have been applied to spectral and temporal\nInstead, they form a distinct affective category that requires the EEG representations [13], while attention-based models have\ntwo poles to be evaluated independently [2]. Instruments such enabled longer-range dependencies to be modeled within\nas the Positive and Negative Affect Schedule [3] and valence- EEG sequences [14]. Affective responses, however, do not\narousal-dominance ratings [4] are commonly used to assess evolve at a single temporal resolution. They include both\nsubjective affective experience. However, reconstructing the short, transient reactions and sustained dynamics throughout\nconflicting components of a mixed affective state after the event the full stimulus duration. The analysis window therefore\nis difficult, and retrospective self-report becomes unreliable remains a central design choice, as it determines the temporal\nwhen affective states must be monitored continuously [5].\ninformation exposed to the model [11]. Mixed emotional states,\nAbstract—Mixed emotions represent a clinically relevant but\nstill underexplored target for automatic emotion recognition. EEG\nprovides millisecond-level access to neural activity, yet most EEG\npipelines analyze the signal through a single temporal window,\nthereby fixing the temporal structure available to the model. This\nstudy introduces a multi-scale temporal framework for EEGbased emotion recognition. The EEG waveform is decomposed\ninto windows of one or several durations, processed by a shared\nattention-based encoder, and integrated through a dynamic fusion\nmodule that assigns sample-specific weights across temporal\nscales. The framework is evaluated under a subject-independent\nprotocol in binary and three-class settings, with the three-class\ntask including the mixed affective category. The best results are\n65.22% for the two-class task and 45.43% for the three-class task.\nBoth are obtained with three-scale dynamic-fusion configurations\nand remain substantially above the full-signal baseline. The bestperforming temporal scales differ between the two tasks. Dynamic\nfusion outperforms concatenation in the highest-scoring two-class\nconfiguration and slightly exceeds it in the highest-scoring threeclass configuration, although these multi-scale settings require\nsubstantially more computation than the full-signal baseline.\nIndex Terms—Electroencephalogram, emotion assessment, affective computing, attention\n\n\fwhere positive and negative components co-occur, remain\ncomparatively underexplored from a computational perspective\ndespite their clinical relevance [1].\nThis study proposes a multi-scale temporal framework for\nEEG-based emotion recognition. Time-domain EEG waveforms\nare decomposed into windows of multiple durations, encoded\nby a shared attention-based model, and integrated through\na dynamic fusion mechanism that assigns sample-adaptive\nweights across temporal scales. The framework is evaluated in\nbinary and three-class classification settings covering positive,\nnegative, and mixed affective categories.\n\ndataset and the affective dimension, with optimal windows\nranging from a few seconds to more than ten seconds [28].\nBeyond fixed-window analysis, multi-scale architectures have\nbeen developed to represent short-term and sustained EEG\ndynamics within a single model. Multi-scale convolutional and\ngraph-based models encode EEG activity at multiple temporal\nresolutions [29], [30], based on the premise that affective\nprocesses unfold across multiple temporal scales. A related\nmulti-window fusion strategy has also been explored for pain\nrecognition from respiratory signals, where a single crossattention transformer aggregates representations extracted at\ndifferent temporal resolutions [31]. Unlike that approach, which\napplies multi-window modeling to a single respiration signal\nII. R ELATED W ORK\nfor pain recognition, the present framework operates directly\nAutomatic emotion recognition has been studied across phys- on multichannel EEG waveforms, processes variable-length\niological and behavioral modalities, including electrodermal windows through a shared multi-layer segment-based encoder,\nactivity, photoplethysmography, cardiovascular signals, and and uses sample-adaptive fusion to weight temporal scales and\nfacial video [15]. EEG occupies a particular role in this setting positions for each recording. Despite these developments, most\nbecause it records central nervous system activity at millisecond EEG-based emotion recognition studies still focus on standard\nresolution and provides access to affective correlates that discrete or dimensional categories. The co-occurrence